  "content": {
    "id": "p7-p1-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "$$\\log_5(y)=3-2\\log_5(x).$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p7-p1-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Make $y$ the subject.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=\\dfrac{125}{x^2}$",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\log_5(y)&=3-2\\log_5(x)\\\\\\log_5(y)&=\\log_5(125)-\\log_5(x^2)\\\\\\log_5(y)&=\\log_5\\left(\\frac{125}{x^2}\\right).\\end{align*}$$\nThe arguments of equal logarithms with the same base are equal, so $y=\\dfrac{125}{x^2}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-p1-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "What is the domain of $y$ as a function of $x$?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x>0$",
          "worked": "The argument of $\\log_5(x)$ must be positive. For every $x>0$, $y=\\dfrac{125}{x^2}>0$, so $\\log_5(y)$ is also defined. The domain is $x>0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      }
    ]
  },
